            "text": "At the edge of town, where the last bus sighs before turning back toward the center, there is a narrow public library with blue doors. Few people notice its garden. It grows behind the building, hidden by brick walls and a row of stubborn hedges.\n\nEvery Thursday evening, Mara, the librarian, carries a dented kettle outside and makes tea for anyone who arrives. There are no invitations, no speeches, and no official purpose. Neighbors bring whatever they have: mint, cardamom, chipped cups, unfinished arguments, or news from relatives far away. Children draw planets in chalk across the paving stones. Retired mechanics debate birds. Teenagers pretend not to listen, then remember everything.\n\nOne autumn night, the electricity failed across the district. Windows vanished, traffic lights went dark, and phones became mirrors. Yet the garden slowly filled. Someone found candles in the library's emergency cupboard. Someone else produced a loaf of bread. In the uncertain light, strangers recognized one another without uniforms, counters, or errands between them.\n\nMara opened a book of poems, but she did not read for long. The wind kept turning the pages, choosing its own lines. After a while, people began describing the places they had once called home. Each memory seemed to warm the air.\n\nBy midnight, power returned with a sudden orange glare. Nobody cheered. They blinked at the lamps, gathered their cups, and promised to come again.\n\nThe garden is still easy to miss. Perhaps that is part of its gift: a quiet place waiting nearby."
          }
